    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "It is demonstrated through example that the concepts of differential geometry find application in the study of joint morphology. Main joint surfaces generally are thought of as planar and it has been proposed that twist hackle resemble helicoidal surfaces. Helicoidal joint surfaces are described by measuring their width, twist angle and rate of twist. Such surfaces have equal and opposite principal curvatures, zero mean curvature, and negative Gaussian curvature. Helicoidal fractures have the property that n such fractures, each of width b, sweep out less surface area than one fracture of width nb, and this explains, in part, the breakdown of joints into hackle during mixed mode I-III loading. To provide an example, the surface of a joint in chert was scanned in 3D and the resulting data analyzed using differential geometry. Although the main joint surface is approximately planar it is composed of low amplitude undulations with elliptical and hyperbolic forms. The unit normal vector does not vary by more than 3.3o over this surface, which is consistent with the threshold angle for breakdown based on the development of fracture lances in glass laboratory specimens. The hackle have approximately helicoidal shapes but only in the breakdown zone. Beyond this zone the rate of twist goes to zero and the hackle surfaces have low amplitude undulations that are elliptical and hyperbolic in form.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
